base.css:232 `.doc code { font: 400 0.9em / 1 … }` and :237 `.doc sup { font-size: 0.75em }`. The comment beside these rules says the floor holds at the 16px body. It does not hold in the design system's own smaller contexts. Measured at 390 and 1440 on a temp article.doc page that loads the built global CSS: code inside `.table-scroll th`, `.callout-title`, `.fig-label` and `.fig-source` renders at 10.8px; sup inside `td`, `.callout[data-callout="tool"]` and `.footnotes` renders at 11.25px. This breaks rule 4. No built page has these combinations yet, because no document pages exist, so the gates are green today. The first project page with a footnote ref in a table cell or inline code in a caption or table header will fail. Suggested fix: `max(12px, 0.9em)` and `max(12px, 0.75em)`.
[also reported by another lens:] base.css:232 sets `.doc code { font: 400 0.9em … }` and base.css:237 sets `.doc sup { font-size: 0.75em }`. The comment on the latter says "12px at the 16px body: the floor holds". It holds only at a 16px body. Measured on the fixture:
- a footnote reference in a right-aligned (mono 13px) table cell renders at 9.8px;
- inline code in a table head (12px) renders at 10.8px;
- a reference in a left-aligned cell, in .footnotes, or in a t-small callout is 15 × 0.75 = 11.25px.
All of these fail G-01 and rule 4. lint:css checks only px and rem (FONT_DECL/SIZE), so it cannot see em sizes. Project pages with sourced tables will hit this.

The tight form `style={{color:'red'}}` in a .tsx file IS caught.
False negatives in the source scan:
- a spaced JSX/Astro attribute, `style = {{ color: 'red' }}` or `<div style = "color: red">` (valid syntax; code files use STYLE_TIGHT, lines 58–59);
- JSX spread props, `{...{style:{…}}}`;
- `h('div', { style: {…} })`;
- uppercase `STYLE="…"` in .md. LEAD is case-sensitive, and the attribute reaches dist unchanged.
The built-page gate L-04 parses with DOMParser and does catch the SSR'd cases. Island states that are only set client-side are seen by neither check.
False positive: escaped text inside a fenced block (`&lt;b style="color:red"&gt;` in <code>) fails the lint, both in the .md source and in dist. An HTML or CSS example in an essay or the kitchen sink therefore blocks `npm run lint`.

i18n.ts:160–195 keeps:
- a second month table, `localDate`;
- an `import.meta.glob(['./format.mjs','./format.ts'])` fallback;
- `usesSharedFormatter`, which nothing reads;
- a second NBSP export.
The fallback dates from before format.mjs existed. Now that it does, localDate never runs, yet it would silently mask a broken import. Separately, content.config.ts redefines LANGS/Lang (also in site.ts) and Status (= StatusState in i18n.ts), so two vocabularies can drift.
